{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-12980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The equation ${a}^{2}+8a=18$ can be written in the form $(a+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-12980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+8a & =18 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a & =18 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2}-16 & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2} & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=34,$ and $p+q=38.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4)^{2} & =34 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{34} \\\\ |a+4| & =\\sqrt{34} \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{34} \\\\ a & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-12980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-12980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-12980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "p+q=-35,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}"] ["math" "p+q=-38,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{35}"] ["math" "p+q=35,\\,a=-4-\\sqrt{34}"] ["math" "p+q=38,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}"] ["math" "p+q=38,\\,a=4+\\sqrt{34}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-12980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p+q=38,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-49190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-49190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=3\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{2j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-49190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-49190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-49190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{2j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{\\frac{j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-49190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{2j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-98013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{2x}-2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-98013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To plot $y=\\frac{5}{2x}-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{2x}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units down to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{2x}-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/08925ef549329dc44ae8dc1711f101d6a2ffbbfa2c4fa225a6d21dccc458bb00.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-98013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-98013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-98013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/08925ef549329dc44ae8dc1711f101d6a2ffbbfa2c4fa225a6d21dccc458bb00.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/259f3b708829f6a814bc4937b44349b91e55ec5b4fb0f73d39b4c993287dabbd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/626fbb611d42800337f0b512cdb0e221e64808ec22e0b434c3bf3c1e6d9386cc.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/9c7701cbc6cdca05f3feed2c006837f5d057196b4617d1b5bb507b85ada04a68.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/a64e70fd5a6680fbf2ccb3236e64cbc51a014e9eecf6596d418e5a18a34fdfe6.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-98013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/08925ef549329dc44ae8dc1711f101d6a2ffbbfa2c4fa225a6d21dccc458bb00.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-112063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider an ellipse such that its major axis $a$ can vary while its minor axis is fixed. What is the best interpretation of ${S}^{′}(a)$ if $S$ is the area enclosed by the ellipse?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-112063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $S(a)$ gives the area of the ellipse with major axis $a.$ So,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}a}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }S}{\\text{Change in }a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${S}^{′}(a)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the major axis changes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-112063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-112063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-112063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The area of an ellipse with major axis $a$"] ["text" "The major axis of an ellipse with area S"] ["text" "The rate at which the area changes as the major axis changes"] ["text" "The rate at which the major axis changes as the minor axis changes"] ["text" "The rate at which the perimeter changes as the major axis changes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-112063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The rate at which the area changes as the major axis changes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-38610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/7c7a636ec47a5daaa64a9464dd428d74eae0feb6369756e741e863aef56048f7.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above can be the graph of the polynomial $y=-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-38610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x\n$$\n\nThe degree $(n=4)$ is even and the leading coefficient $({a}_{4}=-3)$ is negative. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nNone of the graphs satisfy these conditions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-38610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-38610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-38610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A"] ["math" "B"] ["math" "C"] ["math" "D"] ["text" "None of the given options is correct"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-38610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the given options is correct"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-19122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $a=⟨-1,3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨5,1,4⟩.$ Find $3a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-19122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute $3a+b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =3\\cdot ⟨-1,3,2⟩+⟨5,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,9,6⟩+⟨5,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,10,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-19122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-19122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-19122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-8,10,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,10,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,10,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,8,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨8,8,10⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-19122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨2,10,10⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-170593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/319245f467f8512e0fd1d420364a3a88774eda3c9c17c03d4cba6b073c86ad9e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-170593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, $c=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-170593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-170593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-170593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+\\frac{5}{6}"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-170593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-211467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-211467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the left, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(1,-4),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+2)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(1,-4).$\n\n- Then, we translate the graph by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1-2,-4)=(-1,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-1\\cdot (-4))=(-1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,4+4)=(-1,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ is $(-1,8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-211467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-211467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-211467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,0)"] ["math" "(-1,8)"] ["math" "(2,0)"] ["math" "(2,5)"] ["math" "(3,8)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-211467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-1,8)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-48074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-48074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a vertical asymptote exactly at $x=1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-48074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ed6965451d9e7da1315a373f98441fa57d1063a27adfb1367afa212e50d4239"} {"question" "q-48074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1a631a0d5a686d91ab0be44164d772b7777953c58984cb138a00e160db310cb"} {"question" "q-48074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/455cdbecad3ae0949462ee4c75a0dd9d4a199d3a2ac5f1546ced23dfda28d0ec.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/5cebe6d18711ccc3b766388cdd45e6c876be31339fdb5b8fea0fed47cfef9388.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/a9d867bd48a75348b681cea07bb5e1783c9e90e7c4b16e5cda1b6ad377658653.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/d366ad5839b79b71bb16f7d00b98232623a58f980da77065767782348e8b7286.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/d9185fb471c9806d490133c6ea5a54ef69878f89ca0e0be66008ec3626bfe346.png"]] "category"